  "published": "2026-09-26T16:05:56.63Z",
  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "- New ResetHardwareIdAsync: instant self-service hardware-ID reset, so customers can move a license to a new PC themselves (subject to the app's policy and cooldown). RequestHardwareResetAsync is now [Obsolete]: the dashboard does not show those requests yet.\n- Security: the encrypted endpoints now reject a plain (unencrypted) reply that claims success with the new PwfSecurityException, so a proxy or fake server can no longer answer \"success\" for the license server. Plain failure replies are still returned as failed responses.\n- Security: the kill switch can no longer be dodged by changing the clock. The heartbeat used to shrug off plain refusals, including the server's replay check for a clock more than 5 minutes off, so a moved clock kept the app running and deaf to bans. Now only encrypted replies count as answers: MaxHeartbeatFailures beats in a row without one end the session with the new CLOCK_SKEW (clock to blame) or NETWORK_LOST. Plain HTTP 429 has its own budget, the new PwfClientOptions.MaxRateLimitedBeats (default 10).\n- SessionEnded is raised on the SynchronizationContext that started the heartbeat (the UI thread in WinForms/WPF). Opt out with PwfClientOptions.RaiseEventsOnCapturedContext = false.\n- Every request sends User-Agent: PWFAuth-dotnet/1.1.0 (+https://pwfauth.com).\n- New PwfErrorCodes: DeviceLimit, OpenAccessLimit, KeyNotActive, NoHwid, RateLimited, SelfResetDisabled, ClockSkew.\n- A caller-supplied HttpClient is no longer modified; set its Timeout yourself (PwfClientOptions.Timeout applies to the client's own HttpClient).\n- Docs: LogoutAsync ends the session but the key stays bound to the machine (earlier docs said it freed the device seat).\nFull history: https://github.com/pwfauth/pwfauth-dotnet/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md",
